                "content": "The best way to master **Common mistakes** is deliberate variation. Do not stop after making one example work. Change the input shape, boundary, failure mode, and data size, then predict the result before executing.\n\nA strong practice cycle is: define the expected behavior, implement the smallest solution, test normal input, test boundaries, inspect diagnostics, and refactor only after correctness is established. Keep a short note of the bug or misconception you discovered; those notes become an interview revision guide.\n\nWhen a topic connects to another C concept, practice them together. For example, combine arrays with functions, strings with bounds checking, pointers with ownership, and files with error handling. Integrated exercises expose gaps that isolated syntax drills often hide."
