                "title": "Deep mental model",
                "content": "The mental model for **Why conditions exist** is a partition of program states into paths. Start by writing the business rule in plain language, including boundary cases, then translate each rule into a C expression.\n\nRemember that C conditions use scalar values: zero is false and a nonzero value is true. `&&` and `||` short-circuit, so the right-hand operand may not be evaluated. This can be useful for guarding an operation, but it also means evaluation order is part of the behavior.\n\nPrefer clear conditions over dense expressions. Named Boolean variables, early validation, and explicit parentheses often make intent easier to review. Test equal-to-boundary values and mutually exclusive cases, because these are where decision logic most often fails."
