                "title": "Deep mental model",
                "content": "The core model for **Structured programming** is a data contract: several related fields form one logical value, and the program defines how that value is created, copied, modified, and serialized.\n\nWith structures, fields can have different types and padding may affect the in-memory layout. Passing a structure by value creates a parameter object with the structure's value; passing a pointer allows a function to operate on caller-owned storage. This is a design decision, not merely a performance trick.\n\nFor unions, remember that members share storage, so reading a member is meaningful only when the program's representation rules permit that interpretation. For enums, distinguish the semantic names from the underlying integer representation. For `typedef`, remember that it creates an alias for a type; it does not create a new distinct type."
