                "title": "Deep mental model",
                "content": "For **Why a raw char pointer is not enough**, reason in terms of objects and addresses rather than treating a pointer as a magic reference. A pointer value is an address-like value used to designate an object or storage location; dereferencing is an operation that accesses the object reached by that pointer. The critical invariant is that the pointer must be valid for the operation being performed.\n\nTrace three things separately: the pointer's current value, the object it is intended to designate, and that object's lifetime. A non-null pointer can still be invalid, for example after its target has gone out of scope or been released. Likewise, changing a pointer changes where it points; it does not automatically change the target object.\n\nThis model explains why arrays, function parameters, dynamic allocation, and structures often use pointers. Draw the memory relationship before running tricky code. That habit makes aliasing, mutation, and lifetime bugs much easier to spot."
