                "content": "Treat **String memory model** as an operation on a character sequence with an explicit representation, not as a high-level string object. In the traditional C string model, a character array represents a string only when a terminating null character is present. Therefore capacity must account for both the visible characters and the terminator.\n\nKeep three quantities distinct: allocated capacity, current string length, and input length. Many bugs happen when code confuses them. Library calls that read, copy, concatenate, or compare strings also have preconditions about valid pointers, termination, and destination space.\n\nFor robust code, validate lengths before copying, prefer bounded interfaces where appropriate, and be explicit about ownership of writable buffers. Test empty input, exact-capacity input, overlong input, and strings containing spaces. This representation-first model is more useful than memorizing individual string functions."
