                "content": "For **Debugging files**, think of a file as an external resource with a lifecycle and an error-prone boundary. Opening a stream establishes access; reading or writing transfers data; closing releases the resource. Every stage can fail and should be checked when failure matters.\n\nSeparate the in-memory representation from the serialized representation. Binary data may depend on object representation and portability assumptions, while text formats need explicit parsing rules. Do not assume that one successful read means the requested amount of data was obtained.\n\nProduction-quality file code handles missing files, permissions, partial I/O, malformed input, end-of-file, and cleanup paths. Keep the file handle's ownership clear and close it on every relevant path. For durable updates, consider write ordering and temporary-file/rename patterns rather than assuming that a library call alone guarantees persistence."
