                "title": "Deep mental model",
                "content": "The right mental model for **Debugging loops** is controlled state transition. Every loop should have a clearly defined initial state, a condition that determines whether another iteration may begin, and an update that moves the state toward termination.\n\nBefore coding, decide the first and last valid values. Then test zero iterations, one iteration, the smallest valid input, the largest practical input, and the boundary just outside the valid range. This exposes off-by-one errors early.\n\nFor nested loops, define what one inner iteration represents before analyzing total work. `break` changes control flow by leaving the relevant loop or switch, while `continue` skips to the next loop iteration. Use them when they clarify the algorithm; avoid clever control flow that makes termination difficult to reason about."
